1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,360
Still picking at breakouts even though you have melasma?

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:13,600
Stop right there - that habit, plus applying sunscreen just once a day and scrubbing your skin too hard, is quietly making it worse.

3
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:32,160
Melasma shows up when UV exposure pushes melanocytes to overproduce pigment, and hormones make it worse - melasma affects an estimated 5 to 6 million women in the US, and more than 80% of people with melasma have a history of using oral contraceptives.

4
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:36,640
First, applying sunscreen once in the morning and calling it done.

5
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:43,360
Sun protection fades over just a few hours, so melanocytes keep getting triggered for the rest of the day.

6
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:48,080
Second, picking at breakouts or scrubbing irritated skin.

7
00:00:48,400 --> 00:01:01,280
That friction and inflammation is why 66.8 percent of people with melasma develop dark marks after acne, compared to 24.1 percent of people without melasma.

8
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:11,280
Reapply SPF 30 or higher every 2 hours - a tinted mineral sunscreen with iron oxide blocks visible light too.

9
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:17,520
Swap picking for a pimple patch and a gentle, fragrance-free cleanser instead of a scrub.

10
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:27,600
Citrus, kiwi, almonds, colorful vegetables, and salmon give your skin the vitamin C and E it needs to repair UV damage.
